<title>AUSTRALIA: RTRS-AWB Australian wheat indicator prices - Feb 26.</title>
<headline>RTRS-AWB Australian wheat indicator prices - Feb 26.</headline>
<dateline>MELBOURNE 1997-02-26</dateline>
<text>
<p>Australian Wheat Board international card prices for wheat exports for Feb 26.</p>
<p>In U.S. dollars per tonne, FOB eastern states.</p>
<p>		ASW	  AH     PH 13 PCT  PH 14 PCT</p>
<p>    Feb    204 00    208.50    211.00     221.50</p>
<p>    Mar    204.00    208.50    211.00     221.50</p>
<p>    Apr    202.00    206.50    209.00     221.50</p>
<p>    May    202.00    206.50    209.00     221.50</p>
<p>    Jun    202.00    206.50    209.00     221.50</p>
<p>    Jul    200.50    206.00    207.50     220.00</p>
<p>Note: ASW = Australian Standard White; AH = Australian Hard; PH = Australian Prime Hard; percentages = protein content.</p>
<p>Australian Wheat Board indicator prices for multi-grade contracts for 1997/98 crop wheat remained unchanged.</p>
<p>	  Indicator Prices in A$ per tonne:</p>
<p>		  ASW 10 pct protein</p>
<p>National FOB	170.00</p>
<p>Indicator prices on a delivered port basis:</p>
<p> Queensland			   South Australia</p>
<p>    Mackay	  145.50	    Port Adelaide	 147.00</p>
<p>    Gladstone     145.75	    Port Giles	    147.75</p>
<p>    Brisbane	143.50	    Wallaroo		145.50</p>
<p> New South Wales			Port Pirie	    142.50</p>
<p>    Newcastle     145.50	    Port Lincoln	  148.75</p>
<p>    Port Kembla   146.00	    Thevenard	     143.75</p>
<p>Victoria			     Western Australia</p>
<p>    Geelong	 145.00	     Esperance	    150.25</p>
<p>    Portland	146.00	     Albany		 151.25</p>
<p>					     Fremantle	    153.25</p>
<p>					     Geraldton	    151.25</p>
<p>NOTE: The indicator price represents the current state of the international wheat market. It is a cash contract price quoted on a delivered port basis, offered today by the AWB for wheat delivered during the 1997/98 harvest. The offer is valid until 4.30 p.m. Australian Eastern Standard Summer Time (0530 GMT).</p>
<p>-- Sydney Newsroom 61-2 9373-1800</p>
